/-!
# The vanishing of a deficient product of two closed subvarieties

Let `X` be a nonsingular variety of dimension `dX` over an algebraically closed field `k` — the
convention of Section 43.14, Tag 0AZ8 — let `V = closure {a}` and `W = closure {b}` be two closed
subvarieties, indexed as cycles are by their generic points, and let `n` be an integer. Assume

* every point of `V ∩ W` has relative dimension at most `n`, and
* `dim V + dim W < dX + n`.

Then the Section 43.14 product `V · W` in dimension `n`, the alternating sum
`∑ᵢ (-1)ⁱ [Torᵢ^{𝒪_X}(𝒪_V, 𝒪_W)]_n`, is the zero cycle. With `n = r + s - dX` this is the
complementary case `dim V < r` or `dim W < s` of the dévissage of the Tor formula of Tag 0B0W.

The statement is not formal: already for `X` a nonsingular surface and `V = W` a closed point
three of the `Tor` modules are nonzero, of lengths `1`, `2` and `1`, and only the alternating sum
vanishes.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Intersection.ReductionToDiagonal.torSubvarietyProductOfBound_eq_zero_of_lt` — **the
  vanishing of a deficient product**, the statement above.
* `Stacks.Intersection.ReductionToDiagonal.ker_prodMap_subschemeι` — the ideal sheaf of `X ×ₖ X`
  cut out by the product `V ×ₖ W` of two closed subschemes is `pr₁^* ℐ_V ⊔ pr₂^* ℐ_W`.
* `Stacks.Intersection.ReductionToDiagonal.exists_pullback_subschemeι_apply_eq` — a point of the
  support of `ℐ_V ⊔ ℐ_W` is the image of a point of the scheme-theoretic intersection
  `V ×_X W`.
* `Stacks.Intersection.ReductionToDiagonal.ringKrullDim_quotient_stalk_sup_eq_zero` and
  `Stacks.Intersection.ReductionToDiagonal.ringKrullDim_quotient_stalk_sup_comap_lt` — the two
  numerical hypotheses of the Hilbert-polynomial vanishing criterion, for the pair
  `(Δ, V ×ₖ W)` on `X ×ₖ X`: the local ring of `V ∩ W` at the point is zero-dimensional, and the
  local ring of `V ×ₖ W` at `Δ(z)` has dimension less than `dX`.
* `Stacks.Intersection.ReductionToDiagonal.ringKrullDim_stalk_subscheme_eq_toNat_sub` — the
  dimension of the local ring of an integral closed subscheme at a point is the drop of the
  relative dimension between its generic point and that point, and
  `Stacks.Intersection.ReductionToDiagonal.relativeDimension_genericPoint_of_irreducibleSpace`,
  which supplies the equidimensionality hypothesis that computation takes on an irreducible
  scheme.

## Implementation notes

The product is a cycle, so the claim is proved coefficient by coefficient. Off `V ∩ W`, and at a
point whose closure has the wrong dimension, the coefficient vanishes for formal reasons
(`AlgebraicGeometry.TorFormula.torSubvarietyProductOfBound_apply_eq_zero_of_notMem_inter` and
`torSubvarietyProductOfBound_apply_eq_zero_of_relativeDimension_ne`). At a point `z` of `V ∩ W`
of relative dimension `n` the coefficient is the Tor-formula local intersection multiplicity of
the two local ideals (`torSubvarietyProductOfBound_apply_eq_localIntersectionMultiplicity`), and
that is where the mathematics is.

There the proof is the source's, and follows the proof of Tag 0B0V
(`Stacks.Intersection.Diagonal.intersectionMultiplicity_pos_of_isRegular`) step for step, with
the positivity of the Hilbert-polynomial criterion replaced by its vanishing: the reduction to
the diagonal of Tag 0B0U identifies the multiplicity with that of the pair `(Δ, V ×ₖ W)` at
`Δ(z)` on `X ×ₖ X`, which is regular because `X` is and an algebraically closed field is
perfect; the diagonal of a nonsingular variety is a regular immersion, so its local ideal at
`Δ(z)` is generated by a regular sequence of `dX` elements (Tag 0B0Q); and for such a pair the
multiplicity is `dX !` times the `dX`-th coefficient of a generalized Hilbert polynomial of
degree `dim (B ⧸ J')`, which vanishes once that degree is smaller than `dX` (Tag 0B04,
`Stacks.Intersection.localIntersectionMultiplicity_eq_zero_of_isRegularSequence`).

Both numerical hypotheses of that criterion are read off the delta-dimension rather than from
the dimension formula of Tag 0A21 directly. On a scheme all of whose irreducible components have
relative dimension `n` the dimension of the local ring at a point is `n` minus the relative
dimension there, which is `Stacks.Chow.ringKrullDim_stalk_eq_toNat_sub_relativeDimension`; the
three local dimensions `dim 𝒪_{V, v}`, `dim 𝒪_{W, w}` and `dim 𝒪_{X, z}` are therefore
`dim V - n`, `dim W - n` and `dX - n`, and the additivity
`Stacks.Intersection.Diagonal.ringKrullDim_quotient_stalk_ker_prodMap_add` turns the hypothesis
`dim V + dim W < dX + n` into `dim (B ⧸ J') < dX`. For the zero-dimensionality of
`𝒪_{V ∩ W, z}` the same reading shows that `z` admits no nontrivial generalization inside
`V ∩ W`, since the relative dimension there is at most `n` and already equals `n` at `z`.

The one piece of bookkeeping the development did not have is the identification of the ideal
sheaf cut out by `V ×ₖ W`. It comes from
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.productSubschemeIso`, which exhibits
`(pr₁^* ℐ_V ⊔ pr₂^* ℐ_W).subscheme` as the fibre product compatibly with the two projections;
`pullback.hom_ext` turns that compatibility into an identification of morphisms over `X ×ₖ X`,
and `Scheme.Hom.ker_comp_of_isIso` reads off the two kernels. The point of `V ×_X W` over `z`
comes the same way from
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.isPullback_subscheme_sup`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Lemma 43.19.4 (Tag 0B0W)](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B0W)
* [Stacks Project, Lemma 43.19.3 (Tag 0B0V)](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B0V)
* [Stacks Project, Lemma 43.19.2 (Tag 0B0U)](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B0U)
